  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — frugal means careful with money, which is thrifty. Poor names a lack of money rather than a habit with it, miserable describes a feeling, and simple describes the life rather than the spending.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C — generous is about giving, and its opposite is mean or stingy. Wealthy is about having, kind is close in meaning rather than opposite, and careful is closer to frugal.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the idiom means to avoid coming to the point. Searching the garden is the literal reading, which the paper always offers, and the other two have nothing to do with the idiom.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — affect is the verb and effect the noun, so the verb is required after will. Effecting would be a verb but means bringing something about, which is not the sense here.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — candid means frank and open, which is honest. Rude is a possible consequence rather than the meaning, careless and secret are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — unanimous means everyone agreed, and its opposite is divided. Final, secret and delayed describe other qualities of a decision rather than its degree of agreement.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the idiom means to reveal a secret. Losing an animal is the literal reading, and the other two belong to different idioms.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — principal means chief or the head of a school; principle means a rule or belief. They are not interchangeable, and the third option reverses the pair.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — obsolete means no longer in use, which is outdated. Modern is its opposite, and expensive and complicated describe other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — scarce means in short supply, so its opposite is abundant. Rare is close in meaning rather than opposite, and cheap and hidden name different qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the idiom means to make peace. Hiding a weapon and digging a grave are literal readings, and postponement belongs to another expression.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — meticulous means showing great attention to detail, which is thorough. Careless is its opposite, and quick and generous describe different qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — transparent means see-through, so its opposite is opaque. Clear is a synonym, and fragile and shiny name other properties.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — reluctant means unwilling. Eager and ready are opposites, and angry names an emotion the word does not carry.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — take after means to resemble an older relative. Chasing is the literal reading of after, and following a rule or copying a document belongs to other phrasal verbs.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — ambiguous means open to more than one interpretation, which is uncertain. Clear is its opposite, and lengthy and correct are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — expand means to grow larger, so its opposite is contract. Grow, stretch and spread are synonyms or near-synonyms.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — put up with means to tolerate. Building is the literal reading of put up, and refusing is the opposite of tolerating.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — diligent means showing steady effort, which is hardworking. Lazy is its opposite, and intelligent and wealthy name other qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — hostile means unfriendly or opposed, so its opposite is friendly. Aggressive is a synonym, and silent and powerful are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — call off means to cancel. Shouting and telephoning are literal readings, and postponing is a different decision from cancelling.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the blank needs a noun after no, and effect is the noun. Affect is the verb, affects is a present-tense verb, and effected is a past-tense verb.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — cordial means warm and friendly. Cold is its opposite, and formal and noisy describe manner rather than warmth.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — frequent means happening often, so its opposite is rare. Regular, often and usual are synonyms or near-synonyms.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the proverb means that timely action prevents a bigger problem. Sewing and mending are the literal reading, and wasting time is not part of the meaning.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the sentence needs a verb after please, and advise is the verb. Advice is the noun, advices does not exist, and advised is past tense.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — tenacious means holding on firmly, which is persistent. Weak is its opposite, and the other two describe unrelated qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — ancient means very old, so its opposite is modern. Old and historic are near-synonyms, and durable describes strength rather than age.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — give in means to surrender or yield. Handing in work is give in a different sense, entering a room is go in, and donating is give away.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — concise means saying much in few words, which is brief. Lengthy is its opposite, and confusing and polite are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — praise means to express approval, so its opposite is criticise. Commend and admire are synonyms, and greet is unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — turn down means to reject an offer or request. Reducing the volume is the literal reading, and the other two belong to different phrasal verbs.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — stationery means paper and writing materials, so it cannot describe a building; the word needed is stationary, meaning not moving. The fourth option is not a word.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — audacious means willing to take risks, which is daring. Timid is its opposite, and quiet and careful describe different qualities.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — victory means winning, so its opposite is defeat. Success is a synonym, and battle and glory are related ideas rather than opposites.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — to see eye to eye means to agree. Looking at each other is the literal reading, and quarrelling is the opposite.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A — deny takes a direct object and means to declare untrue. Refuse and reject do not collocate with an accusation in this sense, and refute to is ungrammatical.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — prudent means showing good judgement, which is wise. Reckless is its opposite, and wealth and silence are unrelated.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — rigid means unbending, so its opposite is flexible. Stiff is a synonym, strong describes strength, and straight describes shape.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    B — the idiom means to manage on the money available. Joining ropes is the literal reading, and finishing a task or meeting a deadline belongs to other expressions.
  ]

